Acceptance criteria
AC-001: A permitted user can switch safely and the new location appears consistently in all affected modules.
AC-002: An unassigned location, forged location ID, or missing permission is rejected server-side.
AC-003: Pending offline work, unresolved cart, or concurrent transition cannot corrupt the shift.
AC-004: Stock, queue, sales, reports, vouchers, pricing, affiliates, and audit use the correct context.
AC-005: Desktop and mobile users can identify current context and understand blocked actions.
AC-006: Rollback preserves historical transactions and supports reconciliation.
PLAN-05  Backend Permissions and Security
Backend strategy
Reuse the existing switch-location endpoint and use case. Extend only the missing module scope checks discovered during Phase
354. Keep controllers transport-only and enforce rules in use cases and repositories according to the repository architecture
boundary.
Authentication, authorization, and data scope
Layer
Question
Required check
Authentication
Who is the caller?
Authenticated tenant user, active account, valid session/operator.
Authorization
What may the caller do?
pos:switch_location plus module-specific action permission.
Data scope
Which records may the caller access?
Location grant, terminal assignment, shift ownership, and requested viewing scope.
API behavior
Reuse POST /pos/terminal/shifts/:id/switch-location.
Validate shift ID, terminal pairing, destination location, destination terminal, closing cash, opening float, reason, notes, and
idempotency key.
Derive tenant, actor, cashier, source location, and current shift from trusted session and database state.
Reject client attempts to override protected identity or scope fields.
Return deterministic errors: 403 for authorization, 409 for conflict/state race, 422 for invalid input, and 5xx only for unexpected
failures.
Atomicity and idempotency
Lock the source shift and destination terminal/shift records as required by the existing data model.
Perform close/open transition and audit write in one transaction.
Reuse idempotency keys so retries do not create duplicate transitions or cash events.
Preserve the source state if any destination validation or write fails.
Security checks
ID
Attack or failure
Verification
SEC-001
Forged location_id or terminal_id.
Direct API request with altered identifiers must fail.
SEC-002
Permission without location grant.
User with pos:switch_location but no destination grant
receives 403.
SEC-003
Viewing context used for mutation.
Mutation request using viewing scope is rejected.
SEC-004
Cross-tenant identifier substitution.
Tenant ownership query rejects foreign IDs.
SEC-005
Replay or duplicate submit.
Same idempotency key returns the original outcome.
SEC-006
Sensitive audit/log leakage.
Logs contain IDs and reasons, not passwords, tokens, or
payment secrets.
Backend exit criteria
All protected APIs use trusted scope checks.
Concurrency tests prove no double-open or cross-location shift state.
Audit and reconciliation records are produced for success and failure.
No new API duplicates the existing governed switch flow.
PLAN-06  Frontend UX and Cross Module Behavior
Navigation decision
Place Current Location in the global header on desktop and in the mobile sidebar/menu. Clicking it opens the governed flow. Keep
the detailed Change Active Shift Location form inside Shift as the canonical workflow, not as a second implementation.
