Required GIVEN / WHEN / THEN scenarios
Given a user has access to Location A and B, when they switch with a clear cart and valid cash values, then the new shift and
terminal context are Location B.
Given a user lacks Location B access, when they submit destination B directly to the API, then the server returns 403 and
Location A remains unchanged.
Given pending offline operations, when the user selects another location, then the switch is blocked and no shift or cash state
changes.
Given two terminals target the same destination shift, when both submit, then one succeeds and the other receives a
deterministic conflict.
Given a sale completed at Location A, when an administrator views Location B, then the sale remains attributed to A.
Given a parked sale belongs to Location A, when a cashier works at Location B, then it cannot be silently paid as a B sale.
Test layers
Layer
Coverage
Unit
Scope policy, context reducer, transition validation, formatting, permission decisions.
API
Authorization, validation, idempotency, conflicts, tenant and location ownership.
Database
Constraints, locks, historical snapshots, inventory movements, migration parity.
UI
Selector, modal, disabled states, labels, mobile/desktop responsive behavior.
E2E
Login -> context selection -> shift -> sale -> stock -> report -> audit -> close.
Security
Direct API bypass, parameter tampering, cross-tenant and cross-location attempts.
Recovery
Timeout, retry, refresh, restart, failed migration, rollback, reconciliation.
Performance
Large item lists, reports, location filters, concurrent users, refresh storms.
Acceptance gate
No critical requirement may be marked verified without implementation evidence and verification evidence. A passing component
test does not prove cross-module correctness.
PLAN-09  Rollout Observability and Recovery
Release strategy
Use a server-authoritative feature flag and pilot rollout for one representative multi-location tenant before broader release. The flag
must not bypass backend authorization or change historical data.
Release sequence
Run repository checks, migration rehearsal, security tests, regression tests, and E2E evidence.
Deploy compatible backend and migration preparation.
Enable the feature for internal users or one pilot tenant.
Observe transition, stock, queue, report, permission, and audit signals.
Expand location-by-location or tenant-by-tenant only after the observation gate passes.
Success signals
Signal
Expected behavior
Switch success rate
Authorized valid switches succeed without retries or manual repair.
Authorization failures
Only expected denials; no unexpected cross-location access.
Inventory parity
Stock movements and reports reconcile by location.
Cash/shift parity
Closing/opening values and transitions reconcile.
Queue isolation
Incoming orders appear only for the active operating scope.
Audit completeness
Every transition has actor, source, destination, reason, and outcome.
UX errors
Users can understand and recover from blocked transitions.
Immediate stop conditions
Cross-tenant or cross-location data leakage.
Incorrect inventory movement, cash total, or financial report.
Missing or incorrect audit attribution.
Partial shift transition or duplicate shift opening.
Parked sale or incoming queue appears under the wrong location.
Critical security, migration, or API failure.
Rollback and recovery
Disable the feature flag and restore safe application code/configuration.
Keep additive schema changes in place until written data is reconciled.
Reconcile transactions, stock, shifts, queue records, and audit events created during the incident.
Preserve evidence, document root cause, update the plan and tests, and retry only after corrective verification.
Operational evidence
Capture deployment SHA, application/database version, feature-flag state, migration result, test evidence, metrics snapshot, audit
sample, and rollback readiness before declaring release success.
